.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
.RI [ options ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Four cooperating command\-line tools that take raw paired\-end FASTQ files from
an Illumina NextSeq 1000 and produce annotated somatic variant calls.
They can be run individually or chained by the orchestrator.
.PP
.TP
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
Stage 1. Discovers R1/R2 pairs, optionally merges lane\-split files, and runs
.BR fastp (1)
for adapter removal, poly\-G trimming (a NextSeq two\-colour chemistry artefact)
and quality filtering. Optionally runs
.BR fastqc (1)
before and after. Writes cleaned FASTQs plus a
.I run_manifest_*.json
that the later stages consume.
.TP
.B align_reads.py
Stage 2. Aligns cleaned reads with
.BR bwa\-mem2 (1)
piped straight into
.BR samtools (1)
.BR sort ,
producing sorted, indexed BAMs and alignment statistics. Performs no duplicate
marking, recalibration or variant calling.
.TP
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
Stage 3, and a complete self\-contained pipeline in its own right: QC,
alignment, duplicate marking, BQSR, Mutect2 somatic calling, read\-orientation
(strand bias) modelling, contamination estimation, filtering, COSMIC
annotation and SnpEff annotation.
Individual stages can be skipped with
.BR \-\-skip\-steps .
.TP
.B pipeline_orchestrator.py
Chains the pipeline into a single command \(em three stages for DNA, two for
RNA (stage 2 is skipped and cannot be used) \(em handling the manifest handoff
between them.
.TP
.B pcgr_report.py
Optional reporting step, invoked as step 13 of
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
or run standalone. Wraps PCGR (Personal Cancer Genome Reporter) to turn a
somatic VCF into a clinical interpretation report: therapeutic actionability
tiered by AMP/ASCO/CAP. Tumour mutational burden, microsatellite instability
and COSMIC mutational-signature fitting are computed only when the matching
.BI \-\-pcgr\-estimate\- *
flag is given; PCGR leaves all three off by default and does not warn, so the
report simply lacks those sections.
.TP
.B coverage_report.py
Optional check, invoked as step 12 of
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
or run standalone. Measures a BED target against the aligned reads and reports
which regions reached the depth a call must clear, naming the stretches that
did not.
.B It exists because a VCF cannot say "not sequenced":
an unsequenced region and a wild\-type one both yield no variant, so without
this a capture dropout reaches the clinical report as a negative result.
.
.SS Input modes
The alignment and variant\-calling tools accept sample input three ways, in
priority order:
.IP 1. 4
.BR \-\-manifest :
reuse a previous
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
run. Sample names and cleaned FASTQ paths come from the manifest, and in
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
the
.B qc
step is automatically skipped.
.IP 2. 4
.BR \-\-auto\-discover
.RB ( comprehensive_variant_calling.py
only): find R1/R2 pairs in
.I INPUT_DIR
by glob pattern. The first pair becomes the tumour, the second the normal,
and any further pairs are ignored. Use it only on a directory holding ONE
patient \(em see
.B CAVEATS .
.IP 3. 4
Explicit
.BR \-\-tumour\-sample ,
.BR \-\-tumour\-r1 ,
.B \-\-tumour\-r2
(and optionally the matching
.B \-\-normal\-*
options).
.
.SS The handoff mechanism
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
writes
.IR run_manifest_<timestamp>.json ,
recording the exact path of every cleaned FASTQ. Both downstream tools read it
via
.BR \-\-manifest ,
which avoids fragile filename guessing.
.PP
When the orchestrator runs stage 2, it additionally appends
.B \-\-skip\-steps align
and
.BI \-\-aligned\-dir " STAGE2_OUTPUT" /aligned
to stage 3's arguments, so the BAMs stage 2 produced are reused. Without this,
stage 3 would re\-align from the cleaned FASTQs and BWA\-MEM2 would run twice.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.
.SS fastq_qc_clean.py \(em QC and cleaning
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-input ", " \-\-input\-dir " DIR"
Directory containing the raw FASTQ files. Required.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output ", " \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Directory for cleaned FASTQ files and reports. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-r1\-pattern " GLOB"
Glob matching Read 1 files. Default:
.IR *_R1_*.fastq* .
.TP
.BI \-\-r2\-pattern " GLOB"
Glob matching Read 2 files. Default:
.IR *_R2_*.fastq* .
The two patterns must differ in exactly one literal segment; that segment is
the read token used to pair files.
.TP
.B \-\-recursive
Search subdirectories recursively.
.TP
.B \-\-merge\-lanes
Concatenate lane\-split files
.RI ( _L001 .. _L004 )
into one sample before cleaning. See
.B CAVEATS
regarding sample naming.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Reprocess samples whose cleaned output already exists. By default such samples
are skipped and recorded with status
.IR skipped_existing .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the commands without running fastp or FastQC.
.TP
.BI \-\-average\-qual " INT"
Drop a read whose
.I mean
base quality is below this (fastp
.BR \-e ).
0 disables. Default: 20.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-base\-quality ", " \-\-qualified\-quality " INT"
Phred score at or above which a base counts as "qualified" (fastp
.BR \-q ).
This is
.I not
a mean\-quality filter; it feeds
.BR \-\-max\-unqualified\-pct .
Default: 15.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-unqualified\-pct ", " \-\-unqualified\-percent " INT"
Drop a read if more than this percentage of its bases are unqualified (fastp
.BR \-u ).
Default: 40.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-read\-length ", " \-\-min\-length " INT"
Minimum read length after trimming. Default: 50.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-n\-bases ", " \-\-n\-base\-limit " INT"
Drop a read with more than this many N bases. Default: 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-adapter\-r1 " SEQ"
.TQ
.BI \-\-adapter\-r2 " SEQ"
Explicit adapter sequences. Omit to rely on detection.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-detect\-adapter\-pe
Disable fastp's paired\-end adapter detection. It is ON by default: overlap
analysis alone misses read\-through on long\-insert fragments.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-poly\-g
Disable poly\-G trimming. ON by default because the NextSeq 1000 uses
two\-colour chemistry, where "no signal" reads as G.
.TP
.BI \-\-poly\-g\-min\-len " INT"
Minimum poly\-G run length to trim. Default: 10.
.TP
.B \-\-cut\-right
Enable 3\(aq sliding\-window quality trimming. OFF by default: it distorts
allele fractions at read ends in low\-VAF somatic calling.
.TP
.BI \-\-cut\-right\-mean\-quality " INT"
Sliding\-window mean quality when
.B \-\-cut\-right
is set. Default: 20.
.TP
.B \-\-correction
Enable overlap\-based base correction. OFF by default: it interferes with UMI
consensus calling.
.TP
.BI \-\-umi\-loc " LOC"
Where the UMI lives: one of
.IR index1 ", " index2 ", " read1 ", " read2 ", " per_index ", " per_read .
If your panel carries UMIs and you omit this, the UMI bases are consumed as
insert sequence and downstream consensus calling is invalid.
.TP
.BI \-\-umi\-len " INT"
UMI length in bases. Required when
.B \-\-umi\-loc
is
.IR read1 ", " read2 " or " per_read ;
for index\-based UMIs the whole index read is the UMI, so no length is needed.
.TP
.BI \-\-umi\-skip " INT"
Bases to skip after the UMI (for example a linker). Requires
.BR \-\-umi\-loc .
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " INT"
Threads for fastp (which caps usefully near 16). Default: 8.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-fastqc
Skip FastQC on the raw reads.
.TP
.B \-\-fastqc\-cleaned
Also run FastQC on the cleaned reads.
.TP
.B \-\-failed\-out
Write discarded reads to a per\-sample file for audit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-overrepresentation
Disable fastp's overrepresented\-sequence analysis. It is ON by default and is
useful for spotting library artefacts, but costs runtime.
.TP
.BI \-\-reads\-to\-process " INT"
Process only this many reads from each FASTQ. Useful for quick test runs.
.
.SS align_reads.py \(em alignment
.TP
.BI \-i ", " \-\-input\-dir " DIR"
Directory containing cleaned FASTQ files. Required unless
.B \-\-manifest
is given.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Directory for aligned BAMs and statistics. Required.
.TP
.BI \-r ", " \-\-reference " REF"
Reference genome FASTA, or the known name
.I hg38
to download it automatically from the Broad Institute's public bucket. A name
is resolved against
.B \-\-reference\-dir
first, so an already\-installed genome is used as it stands.
.TP
.BI \-\-reference\-dir " DIR"
Shared directory holding downloaded genomes and their indices, consulted
before anything is downloaded and written to when something must be. Kept
deliberately outside the output directory: the reference and its indices are
identical for every run, and putting them under
.B \-\-output\-dir
meant each new output directory paid for its own 3 GB download and its own
1\(en2 hour bwa\-mem2 index. Default:
.IR ~/data/references ,
or
.BR $PIPELINE_REFERENCE_DIR .
.TP
.BI \-\-manifest " FILE"
A
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
run manifest. Sample names and cleaned FASTQ paths are read from it, overriding
any explicit
.B \-\-tumour\-r1/r2
arguments.
.TP
.BI \-\-tumour\-sample " NAME"
Sample name for the tumour, used in the BAM @RG SM tag.
.TP
.BI \-\-tumour\-r1 " FILE"
.TQ
.BI \-\-tumour\-r2 " FILE"
Tumour FASTQs. A bare filename is resolved against
.IR INPUT_DIR .
.TP
.BI \-\-normal\-sample " NAME"
.TQ
.BI \-\-normal\-r1 " FILE"
.TQ
.BI \-\-normal\-r2 " FILE"
Matched normal. All three must be given together, or none.
.TP
.B \-\-two\-pass
Enable BWA\-MEM2 two\-pass alignment. Slower but more sensitive in difficult
regions, which is recommended for tumours carrying structural rearrangements.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " INT"
CPU threads. BWA\-MEM2 uses all of them; samtools sort uses
.IR threads\-1 .
Default: 8.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-stats
Skip generation of flagstat/idxstats/stats files.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-fastqc
Skip FastQC on the aligned BAM.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Realign a sample whose sorted BAM
.I and
its .bai already exist. By default such a sample is skipped, which makes
re\-running the stage after a crash cheap.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print every command without executing it. No alignment, no reference download,
no input hashing.
.
.SS comprehensive_variant_calling.py \(em full pipeline
Accepts, with identical spelling and defaults, every QC option listed for
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
above except
.BR \-\-skip\-fastqc ", " \-\-fastqc\-cleaned " and " \-\-overwrite
(it runs no FastQC and has no output\-reuse detection), and every
.BR \-\-tumour\-* / \-\-normal\-* ,
.BR \-\-manifest ,
.BR \-i ", " \-o ", " \-r ", " \-\-reference\-dir ", " \-\-two\-pass ", "
.BR \-\-threads ", " \-\-dry\-run
option listed for
.BR align_reads.py .
Options unique to it:
.TP
.BI \-\-panel " ID"
Configure the run from a panel profile instead of setting nine flags by
hand. The profile chooses interval padding, the allele\-fraction and depth
floors, whether duplicate marking and BQSR are meaningful for that
chemistry, the UMI layout, and which report statistics the target is large
enough to support \(em each of which is silent when it is wrong. Explicit
flags always win over the profile, and what it applied is printed at the
start of the run and recorded in the manifest. Profiles ship for Illumina,
Thermo Fisher, Agilent, Twist, IDT, QIAGEN, Archer and Roche kits, plus
generic shapes
.RB ( generic\-capture ", " generic\-amplicon ", " generic\-hotspot ", "
.BR ctdna\-capture ", " wes ", " wgs )
for anything not listed. See
.B \-\-list\-panels
and PANELS.md.
.TP
.BI \-\-panel\-bed " BED"
The kit's target BED, used for
.I both
.B \-\-intervals
and
.BR \-\-coverage\-bed .
They are the same file in all but unusual laboratories, and filling only one
of them leaves a run that either calls genome\-wide or makes no coverage
statement. No profile ships a BED: vendor BEDs are licensed,
version\-specific content that comes with the kit. Once a BED is known, its
footprint is measured and used as the TMB denominator (PCGR otherwise
assumes 34\ Mb, so a 2\ Mb panel reports a TMB roughly 17x too low), and its
contig naming is checked against the reference \(em an Ensembl\-style BED
.RB ( 1 ", " MT )
is called over a renamed copy written into
.IR <output\-dir>/panel/ ,
rather than making the coverage report state that none of the panel was
covered.
.TP
.BI \-\-panel\-file " JSON"
Additional profile file or directory, repeatable. Profiles are also picked
up automatically from
.I ~/.config/cancer_pipeline/panels
and
.BR $CANCER_PIPELINE_PANEL_DIR .
A profile whose id matches a built\-in replaces it.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-panels
.TQ
.BI \-\-describe\-panel " ID"
.TQ
.BI \-\-new\-panel\-template " JSON"
List every known profile, print one in full with its caveats, or write a
starting point for a kit that is not built in. Each answers and exits, and
none needs
.BR \-o / \-r .
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-panel\-as " JSON"
.TQ
.BI \-\-panel\-id " ID"
After the settings are resolved, save them as a reusable profile. The
intended workflow is to tune a run with ordinary flags under
.B \-\-dry\-run
until it is right, then save it. Paths are deliberately not stored.
.TP
.BI \-\-mutect2\-extra\-args " STRING"
Extra arguments forwarded verbatim to Mutect2, appended last so they
override what the pipeline built. The escape hatch for a chemistry that
needs a caller setting this script has no flag for.
.TP
.BI \-\-r1\-pattern " GLOB"
.TQ
.BI \-\-r2\-pattern " GLOB"
.TQ
.B \-\-recursive
As for
.BR fastq_qc_clean.py ,
used by
.BR \-\-auto\-discover .
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-discover
Discover FASTQ pairs in
.I INPUT_DIR
by pattern instead of naming them explicitly. The first pair is the tumour, the
second the normal, and any further pairs are silently ignored.
.B This is safe only when the directory holds one patient.
See
.B CAVEATS .
.TP
.B \-\-merge\-lanes
Accepted for interface compatibility only. In auto\-discover mode lane\-split
samples are
.I always
concatenated, so this flag has no effect. See
.BR CAVEATS .
.TP
.BI \-\-dbsnp " VCF"
dbSNP VCF, used as a BQSR known\-sites resource.
.TP
.BI \-\-germline\-resource " VCF"
gnomAD VCF for germline filtering. Important in tumour\-only mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-panel\-of\-normals " VCF"
Panel of Normals VCF for recurrent\-artefact subtraction.
.TP
.BI \-\-known\-indels " VCF..."
Additional known\-indel VCFs for BQSR.
.TP
.BI \-\-cosmic " VCF"
COSMIC VCF of known somatic cancer mutations, used to annotate the filtered
calls. Must match the reference build. Obtain it from
.UR https://cancer.sanger.ac.uk/cosmic
.UE .
.TP
.BI \-\-contamination\-resource " VCF"
Common biallelic germline SNP VCF, with allele frequencies in
.IR INFO/AF ,
used to estimate cross\-sample contamination (step 7). Broad's
.I small_exac_common_3.hg38.vcf.gz
is the standard choice. Without it FilterMutectCalls assumes a contamination
fraction of ZERO rather than measuring one, so real contamination goes
undetected \(em which matters most in tumour\-only mode, where no matched
normal can contradict a low\-VAF call.
.TP
.BI \-\-msi\-models " DIR"
MSIsensor2 model directory for this genome build. Supplying it enables STEP 8,
which scores microsatellite instability from the tumour BAM alone.
.B PCGR will not do this on a panel:
it restricts MSI prediction to WGS/WES tumour\-control runs and, on a targeted
or tumour\-only query, logs a warning and omits the section \(em so
.B \-\-pcgr\-estimate\-msi
is a no\-op there and the report simply has no MSI answer. Upstream calls MSI\-H
at 20% or above for tumour\-only data; the 3.5% figure belongs to the older
paired caller and would call almost anything unstable. The covered\-site count
is reported alongside the score, because a panel carries only the
microsatellites inside its own targets and a thin one moves the percentage by
whole points.
.TP
.BI \-\-intervals " FILE"
Target intervals as .bed, .interval_list or .list, restricting BQSR and
Mutect2. ApplyBQSR is deliberately NOT restricted, since it must rewrite the
whole BAM.
.B Supply this for any capture panel or exome.
Without it Mutect2 traverses the entire genome and calls on the off\-target
reads every capture protocol leaves scattered across it; on a 4.5 Mb panel
that was 635,414 raw candidates and 127,081 PASS calls, against 20,761 and
1,464 with. Use the vendor's BED, in the reference's contig naming.
.TP
.BI \-\-interval\-padding " INT"
Extend each interval by this many bases on both sides. 50\(en100 is usual for
capture: probes cover the target but reads run past its edges, so a variant at
the first or last base needs the flanking reads to be callable. Ignored
without
.BR \-\-intervals .
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-coverage\-bed " FILE"
Target regions checked for coverage before the clinical report is written.
Every region is measured against the depth a call must reach, and the
stretches that fall short are named, base by base, in a per\-sample HTML
report under
.IR <output>/coverage/ .
.B This is the only thing in the run that distinguishes a region nobody
.B sequenced from a region that is genuinely wild type:
both produce no variant, and a VCF has no way to say which happened, so a
capture dropout otherwise reads as a negative result all the way into the
clinical report. Optional \(em without it the run proceeds and simply makes
no coverage statement.
.TP
.BI \-\-coverage\-min\-depth " INT"
Depth a base must reach to count as covered. Default:
.BR \-\-min\-depth ,
the depth below which a call would have been filtered out anyway.
.TP
.BI \-\-coverage\-min\-mapq " INT"
Ignore reads below this mapping quality when measuring coverage, so
"covered" means covered as far as the caller is concerned. Default: 20.
.TP
.BI \-\-coverage\-min\-baseq " INT"
Ignore bases below this base quality when measuring coverage. Default: 20.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-depth " INT"
Tag calls whose tumour
.I FORMAT/DP
is below this as
.IR low_depth ,
after FilterMutectCalls. GATK has no depth filter of its own \(em its
thresholds are likelihood\-based and will PASS a variant supported by two
reads. Records are tagged, never removed. The tumour column is located by
sample NAME, not position. Default: 0 (disabled).
.TP
.BI \-\-foldback\-min\-match " FLOAT"
Tag an insertion
.I foldback
when this fraction of it reverse\-complement matches the flanking reference.
Such insertions are library\-prep artefacts: a fragment end folds back on
itself and is extended, so the read carries a sequence followed by its own
reverse complement, which aligns as an insertion of the reverse complement of
the adjacent reference. Nothing else in this pipeline removes them \(em they
are not strand\-biased, not low\-depth, not in tandem repeats, and reach
allele fractions above 0.3, so no VAF floor separates them either.
.B On by default (0.7),
because the failure mode is silent: on the panel this was developed against,
1,882 of 2,047 insertions were foldbacks, accounting for 206 of 270 PASS
frameshift and stop_gained calls \(em including BRCA1, BRCA2, PALB2, PTEN and
ATM frameshifts that a clinical reporter tiered as oncogenic. Records are
tagged, never removed, and
.I INFO/FBMATCH
carries the matched fraction for every insertion tested, so the evidence is
reviewable. Set 0 to disable. Genuine foldback inversions do occur in cancer
genomes, particularly where BRCA\-mediated repair has failed, which is why
this tags rather than deletes.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-allele\-fraction " FLOAT"
Minimum tumour allele fraction; FilterMutectCalls tags anything below it
.IR low_allele_frac .
GATK's own default is 0.0, i.e. no VAF floor at all. On a panel this is the
main handle on PCR\-slippage indels, which cluster at 3\(en10% VAF and
otherwise PASS in numbers that swamp the real calls. Set it from the assay's
validated limit of detection, not from what makes the output look tidy: too
high and genuine subclonal variants disappear. Default: 0.0 (disabled).
.TP
.BI \-\-snpeff\-memory " SIZE"
JVM maximum heap for SnpEff, as an
.B \-Xmx
value such as
.IR 8g .
The bioconda launcher defaults to 1g, which cannot hold the hg38 database: the
run dies of OutOfMemory partway through, after writing tens of megabytes of
valid\-looking VCF. Default: 8g.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-bqsr
Skip base quality score recalibration. BQSR is also skipped automatically when
neither
.B \-\-dbsnp
nor
.B \-\-known\-indels
is supplied, since it needs known sites.
.TP
.BI \-\-aligned\-dir " DIR"
Directory holding pre\-aligned, sorted and indexed BAMs named
.IR <sample>.sorted.bam .
Used with
.B \-\-skip\-steps align
to reuse BAMs from a separate
.B align_reads.py
run. Defaults to
.IR <output\-dir>/aligned .
.TP
.BI \-\-reads\-to\-process " INT"
Process only this many reads from each FASTQ.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip\-steps " STEP..."
Skip one or more pipeline stages. See
.B PIPELINE STAGES
for the accepted names and what skipping each one implies.
.TP
.B \-\-resume
Reuse steps whose output is already complete in
.IR \-\-output\-dir ,
instead of redoing them. A step counts as complete only when every artefact
it owns is present, non\-empty and (for BAMs and compressed VCFs) indexed, so
a half\-written output from a crash is re\-run rather than trusted. Unlike a
step named in
.BR \-\-skip\-steps ,
a resumed step feeds its OWN output forward: resuming
.B bqsr
hands the recalibrated BAM to Mutect2, whereas skipping it would hand over
the duplicate\-marked one. Refuses to run when the previous run's parameters
differ \(em see
.BR CAVEATS .
.TP
.B \-\-resume\-force
Resume despite a parameter change. The result mixes settings from both runs.
.PP
The PCGR reporting step (step 13) is driven by these, and runs only when
.B \-\-pcgr\-refdata\-dir
is supplied:
.TP
.BI \-\-pcgr\-refdata\-dir " DIR"
PCGR reference data bundle. Supplying it enables the step.
.TP
.BI \-\-vep\-dir " DIR"
Ensembl VEP cache directory used by PCGR.
.TP
.BI \-\-pcgr\-assay " TYPE"
.IR WGS ", " WES " or " TARGETED .
Drives TMB scaling. Default: TARGETED.
.TP
.B \-\-pcgr\-estimate\-tmb
.TQ
.B \-\-pcgr\-estimate\-msi
.TQ
.B \-\-pcgr\-estimate\-signatures
Ask PCGR for tumour mutational burden, microsatellite instability and COSMIC
mutational\-signature fitting respectively. PCGR leaves all three OFF by
default and says nothing when they are off \(em the report simply lacks those
sections, which reads as "nothing found" rather than "never calculated".
.B \-\-pcgr\-estimate\-msi
is honoured only for WGS/WES tumour\-control runs: on a TARGETED or
tumour\-only query PCGR logs a warning and skips it, so the flag does nothing
on a panel.
.TP
.BI \-\-pcgr\-target\-size\-mb " FLOAT"
Megabases actually sequenced, used as the TMB denominator. TMB is a rate, and
PCGR assumes 34 Mb for a TARGETED assay \(em an exome\-sized default that
understates a 4.5 Mb panel by roughly 7.5x.
.B Set this whenever you set \-\-pcgr\-estimate\-tmb on a panel.
Use the panel's target footprint: the same BED you pass to
.BR \-\-intervals .
.TP
.BI \-\-pcgr\-tumour\-site " INT"
PCGR tumour\-site code; site\-specific actionability depends on it.
.TP
.B \-\-pcgr\-lift\-tags
Copy the tumour's (and normal's) FORMAT DP/AF into INFO tags before running
PCGR, and forward the tag names automatically. Needed for a meaningful TMB
from a Mutect2 VCF. See
.BR CAVEATS .
.TP
.BI \-\-pcgr\-tumor\-dp\-tag " TAG"
.TQ
.BI \-\-pcgr\-tumor\-af\-tag " TAG"
INFO tags holding tumour depth and allele fraction, for a VCF you
transformed yourself. Unnecessary with
.BR \-\-pcgr\-lift\-tags .
.TP
.B \-\-pcgr\-legacy\-v1
Emit the PCGR 1.x bundle flag
.RB ( \-\-pcgr_dir )
instead of the 2.x
.BR \-\-refdata_dir .
.TP
.BI \-\-pcgr\-extra\-args " STRING"
Extra arguments forwarded verbatim to
.BR pcgr .
.
.SS pcgr_report.py \(em clinical report (standalone)
Runs the same step outside the pipeline, over any somatic VCF.
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-vcf " FILE"
Somatic VCF to report on. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Directory for the report. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-sample\-id " ID"
Sample identifier used in the report. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-pcgr\-refdata\-dir " DIR"
PCGR reference data bundle. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-vep\-dir " DIR"
Ensembl VEP cache directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-genome\-assembly " BUILD"
.IR grch38 " (default) or " grch37 .
Must match the reference the variants were called against.
.TP
.BI \-\-assay " TYPE"
.IR WGS ", " WES " or " TARGETED .
.TP
.B \-\-estimate\-tmb
.TQ
.B \-\-estimate\-msi
.TQ
.B \-\-estimate\-signatures
Request TMB, MSI and mutational\-signature fitting. All three are OFF in PCGR
by default and their absence is silent.
.B \-\-estimate\-msi
applies only to WGS/WES tumour\-control runs; PCGR skips it with a warning on
anything else.
.TP
.BI \-\-effective\-target\-size\-mb " FLOAT"
TMB denominator in megabases. PCGR defaults to 34 Mb for a TARGETED assay, so
a smaller panel reports a proportionally low TMB. Set it alongside
.BR \-\-estimate\-tmb .
.TP
.BI \-\-tumour\-site " INT"
PCGR tumour\-site code.
.TP
.B \-\-tumour\-only
No matched normal: apply PCGR's germline\-filtering heuristics.
.TP
.B \-\-lift\-tags
Copy FORMAT DP/AF into INFO tags before running PCGR and forward the names
automatically. Writes a new VCF; the input is left untouched.
.TP
.BI \-\-lift\-tumour\-sample " NAME"
.TQ
.BI \-\-lift\-normal\-sample " NAME"
Sample names for the transform, when the VCF carries no
.I ##tumor_sample
header to read them from. A single\-sample VCF needs neither.
.TP
.BI \-\-tumor\-dp\-tag " TAG"
.TQ
.BI \-\-tumor\-af\-tag " TAG"
.TQ
.BI \-\-control\-dp\-tag " TAG"
.TQ
.BI \-\-control\-af\-tag " TAG"
INFO tag names for depth and allele fraction, for a VCF you transformed
yourself. Unnecessary with
.BR \-\-lift\-tags .
.TP
.B \-\-pcgr\-legacy\-v1
.TQ
.BI \-\-pcgr\-extra\-args " STRING"
.TQ
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
.TQ
.B \-\-dry\-run
As above;
.B \-\-log
appends command output to a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-panel " ID"
.TQ
.BI \-\-panel\-bed " BED"
.TQ
.BI \-\-panel\-file " JSON"
.TQ
.B \-\-list\-panels
.TQ
.BI \-\-describe\-panel " ID"
Take the assay type and the estimate toggles from a panel profile, and
measure the TMB denominator from the target BED.
.B \-\-panel\-bed
is the one that matters most: PCGR assumes 34\ Mb for a
.B TARGETED
assay unless told otherwise and says nothing about having done so, which on
a 2\ Mb panel is a TMB roughly 17x too low printed in a clinical report as a
number. The measured footprint is used only when
.B \-\-effective\-target\-size\-mb
was not given, because a figure somebody typed \(em callable bases from a
validation, say \(em beats a raw BED footprint. A profile's nominal size is
compared against the measurement and a disagreement is reported.
.PP
Exit status is three\-valued here:
.B 0
success,
.B 1
PCGR ran and failed,
.B 2
PCGR was skipped (not installed, or bundle missing), so a caller can tell the
two apart.
.
.SS coverage_report.py \(em target coverage (standalone)
Runs the same check outside the pipeline, over any BAM.
.TP
.BI \-\-bam " FILE"
Analysis\-ready alignments (post\-BQSR, or whatever the calls were made from).
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-bed " FILE"
Target regions to check. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-sample\-id " ID"
Sample name, used for the output filenames. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Directory for
.IR <sample>.coverage.html ", " .json " and " .tsv .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-depth " INT"
Depth a base must reach to count as covered. Default: 10. Set it to the
pipeline's
.B \-\-min\-depth
so "covered" means "deep enough that a call there would have survived
filtering".
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-mapq " INT"
.TQ
.BI \-\-min\-baseq " INT"
Read and base quality floors, matching what the caller counts. Default: 20
each.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the
.BR samtools (1)
command without running it.
.TP
.BI \-\-panel " ID"
.TQ
.BI \-\-panel\-file " JSON"
.TQ
.B \-\-list\-panels
.TQ
.BI \-\-describe\-panel " ID"
Take the depth, mapping\-quality and base\-quality floors from a panel
profile. The threshold a base must reach to count as covered is an assay
property rather than a universal one \(em a hotspot panel sequenced to 2000x
and an exome sequenced to 100x cannot share one, and the wrong value makes
this report either fail everything or pass everything. This tool's
.B \-\-min\-depth
.I is
the coverage threshold, so a profile that sets only the pipeline's call\-depth
floor supplies it here too, exactly as the pipeline's own coverage step does.
.PP
Exit status is
.B 0
when a report was written and
.B 1
when it could not be \(em no samtools, no BAM, an unreadable BED. The pipeline
treats the latter as a warning: the calls are already on disk, and a missing
coverage statement is a caveat on them, not a reason to discard them.
.
.SS align_rna.py \(em RNA alignment (STAR)
Stage 2 of the RNA branch.
.B bwa\-mem2 cannot align RNA:
a read crossing an exon\-exon junction has no contiguous genomic match, so it
is soft\-clipped back to one exon or discarded \(em silently, and those are
exactly the reads that could evidence a fusion. This aligns with STAR, with
chimeric detection configured explicitly, because STAR does not look for
chimeric alignments by default and a fusion caller handed a default STAR BAM
returns a clean, empty, entirely wrong table.
.TP
.B \-\-build\-index
Build the STAR index and exit. About an hour, ~32\ GB RAM, ~30\ GB disk, once
per genome, annotation and read length. Needs
.BR \-\-reference ", " \-\-gtf " and " \-\-read\-length .
.TP
.BI \-\-star\-index " DIR"
The index directory. Omitted along with
.BR \-\-read\-length ,
an installed index is DISCOVERED under
.B \-\-reference\-dir
rather than guessed: one is used and named, several are listed so you can
be explicit. Shared between runs like the bwa\-mem2 index, so it
belongs under
.B \-\-reference\-dir
rather than in an output directory. The index bakes in the ANNOTATION and the
READ LENGTH; this script records both when it builds one and warns when a
later run's differ, because an index built for 100\ bp reads works on
150\ bp reads and quietly loses junction sensitivity.
.TP
.BI \-\-gtf " FILE"
GENCODE annotation. Use the primary\-assembly GTF: it names contigs the UCSC
way
.RB ( chr1 ", " chrM ),
matching the installed hg38. An Ensembl GTF
.RB ( 1 ", " MT )
builds an index over nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-length " N"
Library read length. Sets STAR's
.B \-\-sjdbOverhang
(read length minus one) when building, and is checked against the index's
build record when aligning.
.TP
.B \-\-sorted\-bam
Also write a coordinate\-sorted, indexed copy. The fusion caller wants the
UNSORTED BAM \(em it reads mates together \(em so this is for QC, IGV and
anything needing random access.
.TP
.BI \-\-star\-extra\-args " STRING"
Forwarded verbatim to STAR, appended after the chimeric\-detection block so
they override it.
.
.SS fusion_calling.py \(em the RNA engine
Six steps: QC, splice\-aware alignment, coordinate sorting, fusion calling
(Arriba), RNA library QC, fusion report. Accepts
.BR \-i ", " \-o ", " \-\-manifest ", " \-\-auto\-discover ", "
.BR \-\-threads ", " \-\-skip\-steps " and " \-\-dry\-run
with the same meanings as the DNA engine, and every
.B align_rna.py
reference option above. Unlike the DNA engine's
.BR \-\-auto\-discover ,
every pair found is processed as its own sample: a fusion is called from one
library, so there is no tumour/normal pairing and none of the ambiguity that
comes with it.
.TP
.BI \-\-panel " ID"
An RNA panel profile. The engine REFUSES a DNA profile by name rather than
running with settings \(em duplicate marking, allele\-fraction floors, target
BEDs \(em that have no meaning here.
.B \-\-list\-panels
marks RNA profiles
.BR [RNA] .
.TP
.BI \-\-fusion\-min\-confidence " low|medium|high"
The bar at which a call counts as reportable. Calls below it are MARKED,
never dropped: a laboratory investigating a clinically expected fusion needs
to see that it was called weakly rather than be told nothing was found.
.TP
.BI \-\-arriba\-resources " DIR"
Arriba's blacklist, known\-fusion and protein\-domain files. Found inside the
conda environment automatically; supply this only when that fails.
.B Without the blacklist
recurrent read\-through artefacts are reported as high\-confidence fusions and
the table fills with noise.
.TP
.BI \-\-rna\-min\-reads\-millions " N"
.TQ
.BI \-\-rna\-min\-unique\-mapped\-pct " N"
The floors the library\-QC verdict is measured against. They differ by an
order of magnitude between chemistries: 2\ M reads is a normal, adequate
anchored\-PCR library and a badly failed whole transcriptome, which is why
they come from the panel profile.
.PP
No duplicate marking, and no flag to enable it. Two reads at one position in
a highly expressed gene are two observations of an abundant transcript, not
one molecule counted twice.
.
.SS rna_qc_report.py \(em RNA library QC (standalone)
Answers the question an empty fusion table cannot:
.B a degraded FFPE RNA extraction produces a clean, well\-formed, empty
.B fusion table, indistinguishable from a specimen carrying no fusion.
Measures input reads, unique mapping, reads lost as \(lqtoo short\(rq, the
mitochondrial and rRNA fractions, and junction and chimeric counts \(em from
STAR's own log, samtools and the GTF, needing nothing else installed \(em and
ends with a sentence about reportability rather than a table of numbers.
.TP
.BI \-\-star\-log " FILE"
.TQ
.BI \-\-bam " FILE"
.TQ
.BI \-\-gtf " FILE"
STAR's Log.final.out, the coordinate\-sorted indexed BAM, and the annotation
(used to derive rRNA intervals). Each is optional; what cannot be measured is
listed by name in the report so its absence is not read as a pass.
.PP
Duplication rate is never reported. On RNA it is not a quality metric: an
abundant transcript produces identical reads legitimately. DV200/RIN is never
computed either \(em it is an instrument measurement of the extracted RNA,
it cannot be recovered from FASTQ, and it is the best single predictor of
whether fusion detection could work at all.
.
.SS fusion_report.py \(em fusion ranking (standalone)
Reorders the caller's table by CLINICAL SALIENCE rather than by the caller's
own confidence, which is a statement about evidence: a high\-confidence
read\-through between two housekeeping genes otherwise sorts above a
medium\-confidence EML4\-ALK.
.TP
.BI \-\-fusions " TSV"
.TQ
.BI \-\-min\-confidence " low|medium|high"
The caller's table, and the reportability bar. Everything is shown; calls
below the bar are marked.
.PP
Same\-gene events are labelled separately.
.B MET exon 14 skipping
is one of the most actionable findings a lung panel produces, it is a splice
event within one gene rather than a fusion between two, and in a table sorted
by gene pair it reads as
.B MET\-\-MET
and is trivially missed.
.PP
The actionable\-gene list is a highlighting aid, not a knowledge base: it does
not claim a marked fusion is actionable in this tumour type, nor that an
unmarked one is unimportant. For a tiered interpretation, this step also
writes
.IR <sample>.pcgr_fusions.tsv ,
which
.B pcgr_report.py \-\-input\-rna\-fusion
reads.
.PP
PCGR 2.x takes RNA fusions as a molecular input in their own right and needs
a somatic VCF only if one is given, so
.B \-\-input\-vcf
is optional: fusions alone produce a report (and need no VEP cache, there
being nothing to annotate), and fusions WITH the matching DNA library's VCF
produce one report covering the whole specimen. PCGR rejects an EMPTY fusion
file, so a run that found nothing writes no input and says why.
.
.SS pipeline_orchestrator.py \(em stage chaining
.TP
.BI \-\-stage1\-args " ARGS"
Arguments forwarded to
.BR fastq_qc_clean.py ,
as one quoted string. Optional when a QC handoff is supplied instead.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-stage1
Do not run QC. Requires
.B \-\-qc\-manifest
or
.BR \-\-qc\-output\-dir .
Takes precedence over
.BR \-\-stage1\-args .
.TP
.BI \-\-stage2\-args " ARGS"
Arguments forwarded to
.BR align_reads.py .
Omit to skip alignment entirely.
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-stage2
Skip alignment; the QC manifest goes straight to variant calling.
.TP
.BI \-\-stage3\-args " ARGS"
Arguments forwarded to
.BR comprehensive_variant_calling.py .
Required. Do
.I not
pass
.B \-\-manifest
here; the orchestrator appends it, along with
.B \-\-input\-dir
and, when stage 2 ran,
.B \-\-skip\-steps align
and
.BR \-\-aligned\-dir .
.TP
.BI \-\-qc\-manifest " FILE"
Reuse this existing QC manifest rather than running stage 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-qc\-output\-dir " DIR"
Reuse the newest
.I run_manifest_*.json
found in this directory.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print each stage command without running it.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-dir " DIR"
Where the orchestrator writes its JSON report. Default:
.IR pipeline_logs .
.
.TP
.BI \-\-assay " dna|rna"
Which stage 3 to run.
.B dna
chains comprehensive_variant_calling.py;
.B rna
chains fusion_calling.py and
.I forces
.B \-\-skip\-stage2 .
That is not an optimisation: bwa\-mem2 cannot align across an exon\-exon
junction, so stage 2 would discard exactly the reads a fusion is evidenced
by, and the resulting BAM looks ordinary.
.TP
.BI \-\-panel " ID"
.TQ
.BI \-\-panel\-bed " BED"
DNA only \(em under
.B \-\-assay rna
it is ignored with a warning, because an RNA run is scoped by its
annotation and fusion_calling.py has no such option. Appended to
.B \-\-stage1\-args
and
.B \-\-stage3\-args
(stage 2 has no panel\-dependent settings), unless those already name a panel
of their own. Both stages must be given the
.I same
profile: a chain whose QC extracted a UMI and whose caller was configured
for a different chemistry is half\-configured, and the half that is wrong is
silent.
.SH PIPELINE STAGES
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
runs fourteen steps. The names accepted by
.B \-\-skip\-steps
fall into two groups, which behave differently when skipped.
.PP
Skipping one of these reuses the previous stage's artefact, which must already
exist on disk:
.RS 4
.TP
.B qc
Use the input FASTQs as if already cleaned. Implied by
.BR \-\-manifest .
.TP
.B align
Reuse
.IR <aligned\-dir>/<sample>.sorted.bam .
Both the BAM and its .bai must be present or the run aborts.
.TP
.B dedup
Feed the aligned BAM onward unchanged.
.TP
.B bqsr
Feed the duplicate\-marked BAM onward unchanged.
.RE
.PP
Skipping one of these simply omits the work:
.RS 4
.TP
.B mutect2
Do not call variants.
.TP
.B msi
Do not score microsatellite instability. Off anyway unless
.B \-\-msi\-models
is supplied.
.TP
.B contamination
Do not estimate cross\-sample contamination. FilterMutectCalls then runs
without the contamination and segmentation tables, exactly as it did before
the step existed \(em assuming a fraction of zero rather than measuring one.
Off anyway unless
.B \-\-contamination\-resource
is supplied.
.TP
.B filter
Do not run FilterMutectCalls; the raw Mutect2 VCF is used downstream instead.
Also suppresses read\-orientation modelling, whose only consumer is the filter.
The
.B \-\-min\-depth
floor is part of filtering, not a step of its own, so skipping
.B filter
skips it too.
.TP
.B cosmic
Do not annotate against COSMIC.
.TP
.B annotate
Do not run SnpEff.
.TP
.B coverage
Do not check the target BED for coverage. Off anyway unless
.B \-\-coverage\-bed
is supplied. Skipping it costs the run its only means of telling an
unsequenced region from a wild\-type one.
.TP
.B pcgr
Do not build the PCGR clinical report. This step is off by default anyway
unless
.B \-\-pcgr\-refdata\-dir
is supplied.
.RE
.PP
Failure of
.BR qc ", " align ", " dedup ", " bqsr ", " mutect2 " or " filter
is fatal, since each produces an artefact the rest of the run needs.
.BR contamination ", " msi ", " cosmic ", " annotate ", " coverage " and " pcgr
are enrichment only: they warn and the run continues.
.
.SS check_db_updates.py \(em database release notifier
With the RNA branch installed it also reports the GENCODE release and
whether each STAR index is still consistent with it. The second has no
other home: an index outliving the annotation it was built from is silent
\(em STAR runs, the mapping rate looks normal, and the junctions it knows
about are the old ones. Both are omitted entirely on a DNA-only machine.
Checks whether any installed reference database has a newer release, and
writes the result to
.I ~/.cache/cancer_pipeline/db_updates.json
for the web interface to display. It
.B reports only:
it never downloads, replaces or modifies anything a run depends on, and no
pipeline stage reads its output. Upgrading a database mid\-project shifts
annotations \(em a new VEP cache changes the transcript set, a new COSMIC
changes identifiers \(em so the decision stays with you.
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-dir " DIR"
Where the databases live. Default:
.IR ~/data .
.TP
.BI \-\-status\-file " FILE"
Where to write the result. Deliberately outside the data directory.
.TP
.B \-\-print
Print a readable summary as well as writing it.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the status as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-write
Check without writing the status file.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Exit 1 when something needs attention. Off by default, so a cron entry stays
quiet on a flaky network. A source that cannot be reached is reported as
.IR unknown ,
never as up to date.
.
.SH FILES
.SS Produced by fastq_qc_clean.py
.TP
.I <output>/cleaned_fastq/<sample>_R{1,2}.clean.fastq.gz
Cleaned reads.
.TP
.I <output>/fastp_reports/<sample>.fastp.{html,json}
Per\-sample QC reports.
.TP
.I <output>/fastqc_raw/, <output>/fastqc_cleaned/
FastQC output, when enabled.
.TP
.I <output>/run_manifest_<timestamp>.json
The handoff manifest: tool versions, exact command lines, input checksums and
per\-sample fastp metrics. Consumed by the later stages.
.SS Produced by align_reads.py
.TP
.I <output>/aligned/<sample>.sorted.bam
Sorted, indexed BAM. The
.I aligned/
subdirectory name is load\-bearing: it is what
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
expects for
.BR \-\-aligned\-dir .
.TP
.I <output>/stats/<sample>.{flagstat,idxstats,stats}.txt
Alignment statistics.
.TP
.I <output>/alignment_manifest_<timestamp>.json
Run record.
.SS Produced by comprehensive_variant_calling.py
.TP
.I <output>/{cleaned_fastq,aligned,dedup,bqsr}/
Intermediates for each stage.
.TP
.I <output>/mutect2/<tumour>.mutect2.vcf.gz
Raw somatic calls, plus
.I .filtered.vcf.gz
and
.I .cosmic.vcf.gz
as the corresponding stages run.
.TP
.I <output>/annotated/<tumour>.annotated.vcf
SnpEff\-annotated calls: gene, consequence and predicted impact.
.TP
.I <output>/pcgr/
PCGR clinical report (HTML plus machine\-readable TSV), when enabled.
.TP
.I <output>/coverage/<sample>.coverage.{html,json,tsv}
Target coverage per sample, when
.B \-\-coverage\-bed
is given: which regions reached the depth threshold and, for those that did
not, the exact stretches a variant could have hidden in.
.TP
.I <output>/{metrics,stats,logs}/
Duplicate metrics, BQSR tables, alignment statistics, per\-sample logs.
.TP
.I <output>/pipeline_manifest_<timestamp>.json
Full run record, including which steps completed.
.SS Produced by pipeline_orchestrator.py
.TP
.I <log\-dir>/pipeline_report_<timestamp>.json
Summary of the chained run. No command transcript is written; each stage's
command is printed to the console as it starts.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A sample failed
.RB ( fastq_qc_clean.py ", " align_reads.py :
one or more samples), or a required stage failed
.RB ( comprehensive_variant_calling.py ", " pipeline_orchestrator.py ).
The orchestrator aborts the chain on the first failing stage, so a later stage
never runs on half\-built input.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line (argparse usage error).
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Full three\-stage run with COSMIC annotation:
.RS 4
.nf
pipeline_orchestrator.py \e
    \-\-stage1\-args "\-i raw_fastqs/ \-o qc_out/ \-\-threads 16" \e
    \-\-stage2\-args "\-o aligned_out/ \-\-reference hg38 \-\-threads 16 \-\-two\-pass" \e
    \-\-stage3\-args "\-o results/ \-\-reference hg38 \-\-cosmic /data/COSMIC.vcf.gz \e
                   \-\-dbsnp /data/dbsnp.vcf.gz \-\-threads 16"
.fi
.RE
.PP
QC straight into variant calling, no separate alignment stage:
.RS 4
.nf
pipeline_orchestrator.py \e
    \-\-stage1\-args "\-i raw_fastqs/ \-o qc_out/" \-\-skip\-stage2 \e
    \-\-stage3\-args "\-o results/ \-\-reference hg38 \-\-cosmic /data/COSMIC.vcf.gz"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reuse a previous QC run:
.RS 4
.nf
pipeline_orchestrator.py \-\-skip\-stage1 \e
    \-\-qc\-manifest qc_out/run_manifest_20260101T000000Z.json \e
    \-\-stage3\-args "\-o results/ \-\-reference hg38"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tumour/normal pair, run as individual stages:
.RS 4
.nf
fastq_qc_clean.py \-i raw/ \-o qc_out/ \-\-merge\-lanes \-\-threads 16

align_reads.py \-o aligned/ \-\-reference hg38 \-\-two\-pass \e
    \-\-manifest qc_out/run_manifest_*.json

comprehensive_variant_calling.py \-o results/ \-\-reference hg38 \e
    \-\-manifest qc_out/run_manifest_*.json \e
    \-\-skip\-steps align \-\-aligned\-dir aligned/aligned \e
    \-\-tumour\-sample TUMOUR_01 \-\-normal\-sample NORMAL_01 \e
    \-\-cosmic /data/COSMIC.vcf.gz \-\-dbsnp /data/dbsnp.vcf.gz
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tumour\-only calling with a germline resource:
.RS 4
.nf
comprehensive_variant_calling.py \-i raw/ \-o results/ \-\-reference hg38 \e
    \-\-tumour\-sample T1 \-\-tumour\-r1 T1_R1_001.fastq.gz \e
    \-\-tumour\-r2 T1_R2_001.fastq.gz \e
    \-\-germline\-resource /data/gnomad.vcf.gz \-\-cosmic /data/COSMIC.vcf.gz
.fi
.RE
.PP
With a PCGR clinical report, lifting the depth/AF tags so the reported TMB is
computed over filtered calls:
.RS 4
.nf
comprehensive_variant_calling.py \-o results/ \-\-reference hg38 \e
    \-\-manifest qc_out/run_manifest_*.json \e
    \-\-cosmic /data/COSMIC.vcf.gz \-\-dbsnp /data/dbsnp.vcf.gz \e
    \-\-pcgr\-refdata\-dir /data/pcgr_bundle \-\-vep\-dir /data/vep_cache \e
    \-\-pcgr\-assay TARGETED \-\-pcgr\-lift\-tags
.fi
.RE
.PP
Resume an interrupted run, reusing whatever finished. Pass the SAME arguments
as the interrupted run \(em
.B \-\-resume
refuses to proceed if they differ:
.RS 4
.nf
comprehensive_variant_calling.py \-o results/ \-\-reference hg38 \e
    \-\-manifest qc_out/run_manifest_*.json \e
    \-\-cosmic /data/COSMIC.vcf.gz \-\-dbsnp /data/dbsnp.vcf.gz \e
    \-\-resume
.fi
.RE
.PP
Report on an existing VCF without re\-running the pipeline:
.RS 4
.nf
pcgr_report.py \-\-input\-vcf results/mutect2/T1.mutect2.filtered.vcf.gz \e
    \-\-output\-dir results/pcgr \-\-sample\-id T1 \e
    \-\-pcgr\-refdata\-dir /data/pcgr_bundle \-\-vep\-dir /data/vep_cache \e
    \-\-assay TARGETED \-\-tumour\-only \-\-lift\-tags
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview every command without running anything. A dry run performs no
downloads, no lane concatenation and no tool execution:
.RS 4
.nf
comprehensive_variant_calling.py ... \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages are prefixed by severity:
.B [INFO]
progress,
.B [WARN]
non\-fatal,
.B [SKIP]
a step deliberately not run,
.B [DRY RUN]
work suppressed by
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
.B [ERROR]
a failure that does not stop the run, and
.B [FATAL]
a failure that does.
.PP
Two warnings are worth watching for. When a
.B \-\-tumour\-sample
or
.B \-\-normal\-sample
name is not present in the manifest, the tools fall back to alphabetical order
and emit a
.BR [WARN] :
without it you may silently analyse the wrong sample as the tumour. When
lane\-split files are detected but
.B \-\-merge\-lanes
was not given,
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
warns that each lane is being processed as a separate sample.
.
.SH TESTING
.PP
.B python3 run_tests.py
runs the bundle's own test suite: 128 checks, about twenty seconds,
nothing to install. It uses only the Python standard library, so it can be
run before the analysis environment has been built. Tests that need Flask
skip themselves and say so.
.PP
No aligner, caller or annotator is invoked. What is checked is what this
bundle decides \(em which settings a panel profile applies, which file the
report step selects, which flags the web interface emits, what a fusion
table says about a result \(em together with one named regression test per
bug that has reached this code. A leaked file handle fails the run.
.PP
Pass a module's short name to run one tier, for example
.BR "python3 run_tests.py regressions" .
See
.I TESTING.md
for what each tier covers.
.PP
.B python3 docs/make_diagram.py \-\-check
verifies that the illustration in
.I README.md
still agrees with the step banners the engines print, and reports any
drift.
.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Python 3.8 or newer, plus these tools on
.IR PATH :
.BR fastp (1)
and
.BR fastqc (1)
for stage 1;
.BR bwa\-mem2 (1)
and
.BR samtools (1)
for alignment;
.B gatk
(version 4 or newer) for duplicate marking, BQSR and Mutect2. Optionally
.BR bcftools (1)
for COSMIC annotation and VCF statistics,
.B snpEff
for gene annotation, and
.B pcgr
(plus its reference data bundle and a VEP cache) for the clinical report;
where these are absent the corresponding step is skipped with a warning
rather than failing.
.PP
.B The Java version matters, and
.B gatk \-\-version
.B will not tell you.
GATK's Spark tools \(em which
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
uses for duplicate marking \(em cannot run on JDK 24 or newer: JEP 486 made
.I Subject.getSubject()
throw unconditionally, and Hadoop calls it while Spark builds its context, so
step 3 dies with
.I UnsupportedOperationException: getSubject is not supported
after alignment has already run. No flag rescues it;
.B \-Djava.security.manager=allow,
the JDK 18\(en23 workaround, is itself rejected from 24 on. SnpEff 5.4
meanwhile requires JDK 21 or newer, so the usable window is 21\(en23. Check
with
.B java \-version
rather than
.BR "gatk \-\-version" ,
which passes either way.
.PP
BWA\-MEM2 needs roughly 32 GB of RAM to align against hg38, and building its
index takes one to two hours. SnpEff needs a heap far larger than its
launcher's 1 GB default for hg38; see
.BR \-\-snpeff\-memory .
.B \-\-dry\-run
requires none of these tools to be installed.
.
.SH CAVEATS
.TP
.B The RNA branch has no validation at all
It is newer than everything else here and has no truth set: no fusion call it
produces has been checked against FISH, RT\-PCR or a second caller. For
anchored\-PCR panels (Archer, Oncomine) the vendor's own caller is the
validated route \(em Arriba and STAR\-Fusion were developed on capture and
whole\-transcriptome libraries, and their assumptions about read distribution
do not hold on AMP data. See RNA_SCOPE.md.
.TP
.B An empty fusion table is not a negative result
Not until the library has been shown adequate. This is the RNA counterpart of
the DNA branch's coverage problem and it is worse, because it is library\-wide
rather than regional. Read the RNA QC report first; it is written before the
fusion report for exactly this reason.
.TP
.B A panel profile is not a validation
The built\-in profiles encode published, nominal figures for one version of
each kit, and conventional rather than measured thresholds. Kits are
revised, laboratories spike in extra content, and a limit of detection
belongs to your validation rather than to a catalogue number. Copy the
closest profile, adjust it to your assay, and keep the copy.
.TP
.B Amplicon chemistry disables duplicate marking, and must
Amplicon reads begin and end at primer coordinates by construction, so
every read from one amplicon looks like a duplicate of every other. With
the step left on, MarkDuplicates flags nearly the whole library and Mutect2
\(em which ignores duplicate\-flagged reads \(em then calls from what little
survived. The amplicon profiles skip it; a run configured by hand must.
.TP
.B UMIs are extracted, not collapsed
fastp moves a UMI into the read name. Nothing here builds consensus or
duplex reads: there is no fgbio step. A kit sold for 0.1% ctDNA detection
will not reach 0.1% here, which is why
.B ctdna\-capture
sets a floor of 0.5% rather than the kit's specification.
.TP
.B Ion Torrent data is configured for, not validated on
The Thermo profiles set what can be set, but the QC, alignment and
filtering defaults throughout were tuned on Illumina paired\-end reads and
the homopolymer indel error profile differs.
.TP
.B \-\-merge\-lanes means different things per tool
In
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
it is a real toggle, off by default, and the resulting sample name
.I keeps
the lane token
.RI ( S1_L001 ).
In
.BR comprehensive_variant_calling.py 's
auto\-discover mode lanes are always merged and the name
.I drops
it
.RI ( S1 ).
Pointed at the same directory without the flag, the two tools therefore derive
different sample names. In the orchestrated flow this does not arise, because
stage 3 always runs from a manifest.
.TP
.B Stage 3 resumes only when asked
.B fastq_qc_clean.py
and
.B align_reads.py
skip samples whose output already exists unless
.B \-\-overwrite
is given.
.B comprehensive_variant_calling.py
does the same only under
.BR \-\-resume ,
never by default. The asymmetry is deliberate: a stage\-3 artefact depends on
the reference, the known\-sites resources and the filter thresholds, so
silently reusing a BQSR BAM built with a different
.B \-\-dbsnp
would assemble a call set out of two different analyses. For the same reason
.B \-\-resume
compares the current parameters against the previous run's manifest and
refuses to proceed when they differ, unless
.B \-\-resume\-force
says otherwise.
.TP
.B FFPE material needs the read\-orientation model
Formalin deaminates cytosine, producing C>T and G>A changes that are damage
rather than biology. STEP 6 exists to catch them, and on FFPE it earns its
keep: on the panel this was developed against it filtered 3,222 calls, 1,824 of
them C>T or G>A. Never skip it on fixed material. Fixation also fragments DNA,
which is why FFPE runs show short inserts and foldback artefacts \(em see
.BR \-\-foldback\-min\-match .
Residual damage still reaches PASS: 59% of C>T/G>A PASS calls on that sample
sat below 10% allele fraction against 37% of every other substitution, which is
the case for
.B \-\-min\-allele\-fraction
on FFPE rather than trusting the raw PASS set. FFPE damage can also widen
homopolymer length distributions, so treat a borderline MSI score from fixed
material with more suspicion than the same number from fresh\-frozen.
.TP
.B Targeted panels need \-\-intervals
Nothing restricts calling to the capture regions unless you say so. Given no
.BR \-\-intervals ,
Mutect2 traverses the whole genome and calls on the stray off\-target reads
every capture protocol leaves behind. Measured on a 4.5 Mb panel: 635,414 raw
candidates and 127,081 PASS calls unrestricted, against 20,761 and 1,464 with
the target BED \(em the difference between a call set you can read and one you
cannot. Neither
.B \-\-intervals
nor
.B \-\-min\-depth
nor
.B \-\-min\-allele\-fraction
is on by default, because guessing a target region or a depth cutoff for
someone else's assay would be worse than doing nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-discover assumes ONE patient per directory
The first pair found becomes the tumour and the second becomes that tumour's
MATCHED NORMAL; anything after is ignored. Nothing warns about either. Point
it at a directory holding two patients and Mutect2 subtracts one person's
genome from another's: their differing germline variants are reported as
somatic, their shared real mutations vanish, and the output looks entirely
ordinary \(em a clinical reporter will tier it without complaint. Give each
patient its own directory, or name the samples explicitly with
.BR \-\-tumour\-sample ,
.BR \-\-tumour\-r1 ,
.BR \-\-tumour\-r2 ,
and use
.B \-\-normal\-sample
only for a genuine matched normal from the SAME person. The
.B webapp/
front end refuses an ambiguous directory outright and offers a batch mode
instead; this command does not.
.TP
.B Large insertions are usually artefacts, not variants
An insertion that reverse\-complement matches its own flanking reference is a
library foldback, not a variant. These survive every likelihood\-based filter
\(em the reads are real and map cleanly \(em and on panel data they can
dominate the frameshift calls, producing convincing oncogenic findings in
BRCA1, BRCA2 and PALB2. See
.BR \-\-foldback\-min\-match ,
which is on by default. If insertions greatly outnumber deletions in the PASS
set, or most indels are longer than 10 bp, suspect this before biology.
.TP
.B PASS is not the same as real
FilterMutectCalls applies no depth floor and no allele\-fraction floor of its
own, so a variant standing on two reads can PASS. On panel data, PCR\-slippage
indels can outnumber genuine SNV calls in the PASS set. If the PASS indel
count approaches or exceeds the SNV count, suspect artefacts before biology
\(em starting with
.BR \-\-foldback\-min\-match ,
which removes them by mechanism and costs no SNVs, rather than
.BR \-\-min\-allele\-fraction ,
which is type\-blind and pays for indel cleanup with SNV sensitivity.
.TP
.B COSMIC and reference builds must match
COSMIC VCFs are build\-specific. Annotating hg38 calls with an hg19 COSMIC file
fails on coordinate mismatches.
.TP
.B PCGR's TMB is unfiltered unless you lift the depth/AF tags
PCGR reads depth and allele fraction from
.I INFO
tags. GATK Mutect2 writes DP, AD and AF as per\-sample
.I FORMAT
fields instead, so by default PCGR has nothing to filter on: it still
produces a report, but the TMB is computed over unfiltered calls and reads
high. Pass
.B \-\-pcgr\-lift\-tags
.RB ( \-\-lift\-tags
for the standalone tool) to copy those FORMAT values into INFO tags
.IR TDP / TAF
(and
.IR NDP / NAF
for the normal) and have the tag names forwarded to PCGR automatically. A new
VCF is written into the report directory; the called VCF is never modified.
Multiallelic records contribute only their first ALT and are counted in a
warning \(em split them with
.B bcftools norm \-m\-any
first if that matters for your TMB.
.TP
.B PCGR does not read the SnpEff annotation
PCGR runs its own Ensembl VEP annotation, so although it executes after
SnpEff in pipeline order it is handed the filtered (or COSMIC\-annotated) call
set rather than the SnpEff output.
.
